WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.383
The incident response lifecycle becomes most useful when teams apply it as an operating routine.

2
00:00:06.533 --> 00:00:12.490
Prepare before incidents happen by defining roles, playbooks, tools, logging, backups, and escalation paths.

3
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.896
Detect suspicious activity through alerts, reports, telemetry, and business signals.

4
00:00:17.046 --> 00:00:23.429
Analyze the evidence to understand severity, scope, affected assets, affected accounts, business impact, and confidence.

5
00:00:23.579 --> 00:00:29.536
Contain safely to limit spread or harm while preserving evidence and considering business continuity.

6
00:00:29.686 --> 00:00:37.771
Eradicate the cause by fixing the system, account, configuration, process, or access path that allowed the incident to continue.

7
00:00:37.921 --> 00:00:42.177
Recover trusted operations through validation, monitoring, coordination, and clear communication.

8
00:00:42.327 --> 00:00:46.156
Document decisions from the beginning, not after memory fades.

9
00:00:46.306 --> 00:00:53.540
Review lessons learned and turn them into improvements to controls, detections, playbooks, access, backups, training, and ownership.

10
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.669
Incident response is not only technical cleanup.

11
00:00:56.819 --> 00:00:59.372
It is coordinated decision-making under uncertainty.

12
00:00:59.522 --> 00:01:06.331
A mature team reduces harm, restores trust, communicates responsibly, and uses every incident to improve resilience.

13
00:01:06.481 --> 00:01:10.311
The routine is intentionally simple because incidents are not.

14
00:01:10.461 --> 00:01:16.418
It gives responders a common language when facts are incomplete and pressure is high.

15
00:01:16.568 --> 00:01:22.526
Used well, it helps a team move deliberately: gather evidence, decide what matters now,

16
00:01:22.676 --> 00:01:28.207
act with authorization, validate the result, and turn the experience into stronger readiness.
